WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.820
Site review combines current official airspace information, permissions, asset drawings or maps, weather, lighting, terrain, obstacles, electromagnetic conditions, and ongoing operations.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:16.831
Coordinate with the asset owner and site-safety lead before route design.

00:00:16.831 --> 00:00:19.476
Structures create unique hazards.

00:00:19.476 --> 00:00:29.334
Wires, antennas, cranes, cables, overhangs, moving equipment, reflected navigation signals, turbulence, and radio shadowing may be hard to see.

00:00:29.334 --> 00:00:33.903
Obstacle sensing cannot replace standoff and visual awareness.

00:00:33.903 --> 00:00:43.761
Choose launch and recovery areas with stable surfaces, clear access, controlled people and vehicles, reliable communication, and emergency options.

00:00:43.761 --> 00:00:50.253
Plan alternate landing areas when the route extends across a large asset.

00:00:50.253 --> 00:00:57.707
The flight path should connect overview coverage with targeted details while preserving safe distance.

00:00:57.707 --> 00:01:07.084
Plan camera angles, route direction, altitude strategy, turns, battery segments, and areas where the aircraft must not enter.

00:01:07.084 --> 00:01:15.981
Crew roles may include pilot in command, visual observer, payload operator, site escort, mission lead, and specialist.

00:01:15.981 --> 00:01:23.434
Use concise communication for hazards, asset references, collection status, battery, signal, and abort calls.

00:01:23.434 --> 00:01:32.812
Document objective, approvals, crew, aircraft, payload, conditions, route, standoff, hazards, controls, emergency actions, data handling, and abort criteria.

00:01:32.812 --> 00:01:39.304
Final go authority remains with the responsible pilot under the operating model.

00:01:39.304 --> 00:01:47.238
Deconflict with maintenance, cranes, lifts, traffic control, rail movement, energized work, deliveries, and emergency access.

00:01:47.238 --> 00:01:54.692
A route approved on a drawing may be unsafe when simultaneous site activity begins.
